00:00:12.201 --> 00:00:14.591
B3, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:14.871 --> 00:00:20.716
Ch 01, Ch 02, Ch 25, Ch 29, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.996 --> 00:00:25.903
The architect's core skill, making, recording, quantifying and governing decisions.

9
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:41.799
Four runnable labs turn decision-making from opinion into evidence: a weighted trade-off matrix with sensitivity analysis, executable architecture fitness functions, a queueing-based SLO decision, and a Monte Carlo decision under uncertainty.

10
00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:45.858
L1: trade-off matrix + sensitivity.

11
00:00:46.138 --> 00:00:54.714
A Modular Monolith scored 4.57 versus Serverless 3.64 versus Microservices 2.86.

12
00:00:54.994 --> 00:01:08.477
Sensitivity showed the choice is robust: tripling the scalability weight didn't flip it; only scalability could flip it at all, at an extreme weight about 8.75, and then to Serverless, not Microservices.

13
00:01:08.757 --> 00:01:10.421
L2: fitness functions.

14
00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:25.591
An eroded dependency graph failed the build with three named violations, an upward layer dependency, a domain, then infrastructure leak, and the cycle OrderService, then Order, then Pricing, then OrderService; the fixed graph passed.

15
00:01:26.941 --> 00:01:28.989
L3: decide with queueing math.

16
00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:43.861
At 800 req per second against a 300 ms p99 SLO, the sequential call chain summed to 334 ms (breach); the parallel fan-out took the max, 111 ms (meets).

17
00:01:44.141 --> 00:01:54.381
The queueing cliff showed p99 going 196 ms, then 16 s as utilization rose from 0.5 to 0.99.

18
00:01:54.661 --> 00:01:55.536
L4:

19
00:01:55.816 --> 00:01:57.928
Monte Carlo under uncertainty.

20
00:01:58.208 --> 00:02:22.272
The point estimate at the likely volume said serverless is cheaper ($23k versus $28k); 200,000 trials over the skewed demand distribution said VMs win in expectation ($29.6k versus $35.9k) and in 64% of futures, with a break-even at ~147M requests per month.

21
00:02:22.552 --> 00:02:23.533
Reading time:

22
00:02:23.813 --> 00:02:25.605
110 min + code.

23
00:02:26.955 --> 00:02:27.809
Part 1.

24
00:02:28.089 --> 00:02:30.009
Introduction and Why It Matters.

25
00:02:31.259 --> 00:02:33.883
A story: the decision nobody could explain.

26
00:02:34.163 --> 00:02:40.136
Two years into the grocery platform, a new principal engineer asked a simple question in her first week:

27
00:02:40.416 --> 00:02:45.045
Why are we running 26 microservices with a team of 18 engineers?

28
00:02:45.325 --> 00:02:46.584
Nobody could answer.

29
00:02:46.864 --> 00:02:49.851
The decision had been made in a meeting three years earlier.

30
00:02:50.131 --> 00:02:51.731
The people in the room had left.

31
00:02:52.011 --> 00:02:59.819
There was a Confluence page titled Target Architecture with a box diagram and the sentence microservices for scalability.

32
00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:01.421
There was no record of:

33
00:03:02.771 --> 00:03:04.713
What problem the decision was solving.

34
00:03:04.993 --> 00:03:05.974
Was it scale?

35
00:03:06.254 --> 00:03:07.363
Team autonomy?

36
00:03:07.643 --> 00:03:10.054
A reaction to a painful monolith release?

37
00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:14.238
Nobody knew, so nobody could tell whether the problem still existed.

38
00:03:14.518 --> 00:03:16.225
What alternatives were considered.

39
00:03:16.505 --> 00:03:19.235
Had anyone evaluated a modular monolith?

40
00:03:19.515 --> 00:03:21.329
If so, why was it rejected?

41
00:03:21.609 --> 00:03:23.230
What trade-offs were accepted.

42
00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:38.209
The operational cost of 26 services, distributed tracing, 26 pipelines, network failure modes, eventual consistency, was being paid every day, but nobody had ever written down that it was the price of some benefit.

43
00:03:39.559 --> 00:03:41.095
What would make the decision wrong.

44
00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:42.825
No trigger to revisit.

45
00:03:43.105 --> 00:03:53.303
The platform had never exceeded 400 requests or second, far below anything that needed independent scaling, but the decision stood because it was never reopened.

46
00:03:53.583 --> 00:03:56.036
Whether the architecture still matched the decision.

47
00:03:56.316 --> 00:04:06.215
Several independent services had grown synchronous call chains and shared a database, so the platform had the costs of microservices and the coupling of a monolith.

48
00:04:07.565 --> 00:04:11.298
The principal engineer's first act was not to redesign anything.

49
00:04:11.578 --> 00:04:20.005
It was to reconstruct the decision, context, options, criteria, trade-offs, and write it down as an Architecture Decision Record.

50
00:04:20.285 --> 00:04:29.074
That exercise alone revealed that the original drivers no longer held, and it gave the team a principled basis for consolidating services.

51
00:04:29.354 --> 00:04:34.965
This chapter is about making decisions that can be explained, quantified, governed, and revisited.

52
00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:36.997
Quote:

53
00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:38.941
The thesis of this chapter.

54
00:04:39.221 --> 00:04:43.125
Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to change.

55
00:04:43.405 --> 00:05:04.269
The architect's job is not to have the best opinions, it is to make those decisions explicitly (with options and criteria), quantitatively where possible (capacity, latency, cost, probability), recorded (ADRs), governed (fitness functions that keep the system true to them), and revisitable (with a stated trigger that would reopen them).

56
00:05:04.549 --> 00:05:08.581
An undocumented decision is an accident waiting to be inherited.

57
00:05:09.931 --> 00:05:12.235
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

58
00:05:12.515 --> 00:05:13.859
Here is a quick table:

59
00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:17.553
Decision practice, and Grocery platform usage, and Lab.

60
00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:19.753
Quality-attribute-driven trade-offs.

61
00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.654
Grocery platform usage:

62
00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:25.774
