84
00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:08.670
Decisions under uncertainty.

85
00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:10.571
Grocery platform usage:

86
00:06:10.851 --> 00:06:15.331
Cost or demand ranges modeled with Monte Carlo, not single numbers.

87
00:06:15.611 --> 00:06:16.294
Lab:

88
00:06:16.574 --> 00:06:17.449
L4.

89
00:06:18.799 --> 00:06:20.121
Review and governance.

90
00:06:20.401 --> 00:06:22.023
Grocery platform usage:

91
00:06:22.303 --> 00:06:26.612
Lightweight ARB for one-way doors; teams decide two-way doors.

92
00:06:26.892 --> 00:06:28.684
Lab: §2.8.

93
00:06:30.034 --> 00:06:31.357
Learning objectives.

94
00:06:31.637 --> 00:06:33.535
By the end you will be able to:

95
00:06:33.815 --> 00:06:40.855
Frame a decision by its drivers (quality attributes, constraints, context) rather than by technology preference.

96
00:06:41.135 --> 00:06:47.855
Score options transparently and run a sensitivity analysis that shows how robust the choice is (L1).

97
00:06:48.135 --> 00:06:54.023
Write an ADR that a future engineer can use to understand, challenge, and revisit the decision.

98
00:06:54.303 --> 00:07:01.471
Turn architectural rules into executable fitness functions that fail the build when the architecture erodes (L2).

99
00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:07.835
Validate a design quantitatively against an SLO using queueing math (L3).

100
00:07:08.115 --> 00:07:13.427
Decide under uncertainty with distributions rather than point estimates (L4).

101
00:07:13.707 --> 00:07:17.653
Distinguish one-way from two-way doors and govern each appropriately.

102
00:07:19.003 --> 00:07:19.878
Part 2.

103
00:07:20.158 --> 00:07:22.035
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

104
00:07:23.285 --> 00:07:26.187
Intuition: what makes a decision architectural.

105
00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:29.325
Not every technical decision is architectural.

106
00:07:29.605 --> 00:07:37.584
A useful test: a decision is architectural if it is expensive to reverse and affects quality attributes across the system.

107
00:07:37.864 --> 00:07:43.773
Choosing a variable name is not; choosing between a shared database and database-per-service is.

108
00:07:44.053 --> 00:07:53.461
Choosing a logging library is borderline; choosing synchronous versus event-driven integration between bounded contexts is squarely architectural.

109
00:07:54.811 --> 00:07:59.057
This matters because architectural decisions deserve a different process.

110
00:07:59.337 --> 00:08:05.822
Cheap, reversible decisions should be made quickly by the people closest to the work, slowing them down is waste.

111
00:08:06.102 --> 00:08:15.339
Expensive, irreversible decisions deserve options, analysis, evidence and a written record, rushing them is how you get §1.1.

112
00:08:15.619 --> 00:08:24.323
Amazon's framing is useful: one-way doors (hard or impossible to walk back through) versus two-way doors (you can walk back cheaply).

113
00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:29.958
Most decisions are two-way doors; the architect's skill is recognizing the few that aren't.

114
00:08:31.308 --> 00:08:34.081
Quality attributes and architectural drivers.

115
00:08:34.361 --> 00:08:40.313
Architecture is shaped far more by quality attributes (the -ilities) than by functional requirements.

116
00:08:40.593 --> 00:08:59.537
Two systems with identical features, customers can place grocery orders, can have completely different architectures depending on whether they must handle 10 or 10,000 orders per second, 99.5% or 99.99% availability, a team of 5 or 500.

117
00:08:59.817 --> 00:09:21.535
The standard set (ISO or IEC 25010 plus the usual operational ones): performance (latency, throughput), scalability, availability, reliability, security, modifiability or evolvability, testability, deployability, operability, observability, cost, time-to-market, and team fit (can the team you actually have build and run it?).

118
00:09:22.885 --> 00:09:26.661
Three rules make quality attributes useful rather than decorative:

119
00:09:26.941 --> 00:09:28.221
Make them measurable.

120
00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:35.285
Fast is not a requirement; p99 300 ms at 800 req per second is (L3).

121
00:09:35.565 --> 00:09:45.741
The SEI's quality attribute scenario format forces this: source, then stimulus, then artifact, then environment, then response, then response measure.

122
00:09:46.021 --> 00:09:46.895
Example:

123
00:09:47.175 --> 00:09:59.847
When 800 req per second of checkout traffic (stimulus) arrive at peak (environment), the checkout API (artifact) responds (response) with p99 300 ms (measure).

124
00:10:01.197 --> 00:10:02.392
Prioritize them.

125
00:10:02.672 --> 00:10:03.696
They conflict.

126
00:10:03.976 --> 00:10:14.728
Maximum availability costs consistency (CAP, Ch 12); maximum security costs usability; maximum evolvability costs time-to-market today.

127
00:10:15.008 --> 00:10:18.528
Weighting them (L1) is how you make the conflict explicit.

128
00:10:18.808 --> 00:10:20.749
Include constraints and context.

129
00:10:21.029 --> 00:10:29.179
Constraints are non-negotiable (regulatory data residency, a mandated cloud, a fixed budget, a team that knows Java).

130
00:10:29.459 --> 00:10:37.544
Context includes the stage of the business, a startup searching for product-market fit should weight time-to-market far above scalability.

131
00:10:38.894 --> 00:10:42.307
The decision process: options, criteria, trade-offs.

132
00:10:42.587 --> 00:10:45.425
A repeatable process for significant decisions:

133
00:10:45.705 --> 00:10:47.539
State the problem and drivers.

134
00:10:47.819 --> 00:10:49.270
What forces are at work?

135
00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:52.814
What quality attributes matter most, and what are the constraints?

136
00:10:53.094 --> 00:10:54.843
Generate at least three options.

137
00:10:55.123 --> 00:10:58.131
Always include do nothing or keep the current approach.

138
00:10:58.411 --> 00:11:02.443
Two options is a false binary; one option is a rationalization.

139
00:11:02.723 --> 00:11:05.411
Define criteria and weights before scoring.

140
00:11:05.691 --> 00:11:10.747
If you score first and weight second, you will (unconsciously) pick weights that crown your favorite.

141
00:11:12.097 --> 00:11:22.359
Score each option against each criterion, ideally with several people scoring independently and then discussing divergences, which is where the real insights are.

142
00:11:22.639 --> 00:11:25.028
Run a sensitivity analysis (L1).

143
00:11:25.308 --> 00:11:27.036
Which weights would flip the outcome?

144
00:11:27.316 --> 00:11:33.311
If a small, plausible change flips the winner, the decision is fragile and you need more information.

145
00:11:33.591 --> 00:11:37.281
If only an extreme change flips it, the decision is robust.

146
00:11:37.561 --> 00:11:48.697
Validate quantitatively where a criterion is measurable, capacity, latency, cost (L3, L4), rather than trusting a 1-to-5 score for something you can calculate.

147
00:11:50.047 --> 00:11:52.799
Decide, record, and state the revisit trigger.

148
00:11:53.079 --> 00:11:54.167
We choose X.

149
00:11:54.447 --> 00:11:56.239
We would revisit if Y happens.

150
00:11:56.519 --> 00:11:58.034
(§2.4).

151
00:11:58.314 --> 00:12:01.578
The weighted matrix is not a machine that produces truth.

152
00:12:01.858 --> 00:12:05.207
Its value is that it makes reasoning visible and arguable.

153
00:12:05.487 --> 00:12:15.130
When two senior engineers disagree, the matrix shows where they disagree, usually on a weight (a values disagreement) or a score (a facts disagreement).

154
00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:22.770
Facts disagreements can be settled with evidence; values disagreements must be settled by whoever owns the business priority.

155
00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:26.765
Architecture Decision Records (ADRs).

156
00:12:27.045 --> 00:12:31.397
An ADR is a short document capturing one significant decision.

157
00:12:31.677 --> 00:12:35.261
Michael Nygard's original format remains the most common:

158
00:12:36.611 --> 00:12:38.190
Here is the MARKDOWN code.

159
00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:42.182
It runs to 33 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

160
00:12:42.462 --> 00:12:43.977
ADR-042:

161
00:12:44.257 --> 00:12:46.838
Use a modular monolith for the order domain.

162
00:12:49.688 --> 00:12:51.736
What makes ADRs work in practice:

163
00:12:52.016 --> 00:12:57.477
Keep them in the repo, next to the code (docs or adr/), reviewed through pull requests.

164
00:12:57.757 --> 00:13:02.515
A wiki page nobody reviews rots; a file in the repo gets diffed and linked.

165
00:13:02.795 --> 00:13:04.843
Keep them short, one or two pages.

166
00:13:05.123 --> 00:13:07.939
The goal is the reasoning, not a design document.

167
00:13:08.219 --> 00:13:09.733
Immutable once accepted.

168
00:13:10.013 --> 00:13:14.259
You don't edit history; you write a new ADR that supersedes the old one.

169
00:13:14.539 --> 00:13:18.421
The chain of superseded ADRs is the architecture's history.

170
00:13:19.771 --> 00:13:21.841
Record rejected options and why.

171
00:13:22.121 --> 00:13:30.057
This is the section future engineers value most, it stops them re-proposing something already evaluated without new information.

172
00:13:30.337 --> 00:13:31.894
Include the revisit trigger.

173
00:13:32.174 --> 00:13:35.737
This single line is what would have prevented §1.1.

174
00:13:36.017 --> 00:13:39.281
Write ADRs for decisions not to do something too.

175
00:13:39.561 --> 00:13:43.635
We will not introduce Kafka yet is a decision with consequences.

176
00:13:44.985 --> 00:14:01.135
Variants exist, MADR (Markdown ADR) adds explicit decision drivers and pros or cons per option; Y-statements compress an ADR to one sentence (In the context of X, facing Y, we decided Z to achieve Q, accepting D).

177
00:14:01.415 --> 00:14:03.655
The format matters far less than the habit.

178
00:14:05.005 --> 00:14:06.306
Evaluation methods:

179
00:14:06.586 --> 00:14:08.463
ATAM and its lightweight cousins.

180
00:14:08.743 --> 00:14:26.450
The Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method (ATAM) from the SEI is the heavyweight, systematic approach: stakeholders build a utility tree (quality attributes, then refined attributes, then concrete scenarios, each rated by business importance and technical difficulty),

181
00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:43.946
architects walk through how the architecture handles the high-priority scenarios, and the team identifies sensitivity points (a decision that strongly affects one quality attribute), trade-off points (a decision that affects several attributes in opposite directions), and risks.

182
00:14:44.226 --> 00:14:51.778
Full ATAM takes days and many stakeholders, appropriate for a bank's core ledger replacement, excessive for most decisions.

183
00:14:52.058 --> 00:14:53.445
Useful lighter methods:

184
00:14:54.795 --> 00:15:05.952
Lightweight ATAM or mini-QAW, a 2 to 4 hour workshop: agree the top 5 quality attribute scenarios, walk the candidate architecture through each, list risks.

185
00:15:06.232 --> 00:15:10.947
Decision matrix + sensitivity (L1), for choosing among a few options.

186
00:15:11.227 --> 00:15:14.448
Pre-mortem, It's a year from now and this decision failed.

187
00:15:14.728 --> 00:15:15.411
Why?

188
00:15:15.691 --> 00:15:19.723
Surfaces risks people are reluctant to raise in a normal review.

189
00:15:20.003 --> 00:15:28.472
Architecture spike or prototype, when a score is really a guess, spend a few days building the riskiest part to turn the guess into evidence.

190
00:15:29.822 --> 00:15:36.755
Risk storming, everyone marks risks on the architecture diagram independently, then the team discusses clusters.

191
00:15:37.035 --> 00:15:48.961
The key concepts transfer regardless of method: sensitivity points and trade-off points are the parts of the architecture to watch hardest, because that's where small changes have big consequences.

192
00:15:50.311 --> 00:15:51.996
Quantitative decision-making.

193
00:15:52.276 --> 00:15:55.860
Where a criterion can be computed, compute it rather than score it.

194
00:15:56.140 --> 00:16:00.663
The architect's quantitative toolkit, most of which appears earlier in this handbook:

195
00:16:00.943 --> 00:16:08.303
Back-of-the-envelope estimation (Ch 02), storage, bandwidth, QPS, order-of-magnitude feasibility.

196
00:16:08.583 --> 00:16:16.903
Latency budgets, sequential calls add; parallel calls take the max; every hop adds network time and serialization (L3).

197
00:16:18.253 --> 00:16:26.850
Queueing theory, Little's Law (L = λW) and the M or M or 1 response time R = S / (1 minus ρ).

198
00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:32.634
The most important consequence for architects: latency is non-linear in utilization.

199
00:16:32.914 --> 00:16:39.378
At ρ = 0.5 you have headroom; at ρ = 0.9 you're on the cliff (L3:

200
00:16:39.658 --> 00:16:46.229
196 ms, then 1,484 ms for a 35 ms service).

201
00:16:46.509 --> 00:16:52.546
This is why capacity targets are typically 50 to 70% utilization, not 90%.

202
00:16:52.826 --> 00:16:59.823
Universal Scalability Law (Ch 25), contention and coherency limit how far adding nodes helps.

203
00:17:01.173 --> 00:17:19.029
Availability math, serial dependencies multiply availabilities (three 99.9% services in series about 99.7%); redundancy improves them (two independent 99% replicas about 99.99%).

204
00:17:19.309 --> 00:17:23.789
Cost models, fixed versus variable cost, break-even points (L4).

205
00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:32.432
Monte Carlo simulation, when inputs are uncertain, sample them from distributions and look at the distribution of outcomes (L4).

206
00:17:32.712 --> 00:17:38.429
L3's lesson generalizes: the numbers often settle arguments that adjectives cannot.

207
00:17:38.709 --> 00:17:52.747
Async is more scalable is an opinion; the sequential chain's p99 is 334 ms against a 300 ms SLO; the parallel fan-out's is 111 ms is a decision.

208
00:17:54.097 --> 00:17:55.761
Deciding under uncertainty.

209
00:17:56.041 --> 00:18:05.790
Almost every architectural decision is made with uncertain inputs, future traffic, future team size, future product direction, vendor pricing changes.

210
00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.286
Three disciplines:

211
00:18:07.566 --> 00:18:09.273
Use ranges, not points.

212
00:18:09.553 --> 00:18:13.307
Ask for pessimistic or likely or optimistic estimates.

213
00:18:13.587 --> 00:18:16.211
A single number hides both the spread and the skew.

214
00:18:17.561 --> 00:18:18.521
Beware skew.

215
00:18:18.801 --> 00:18:24.049
Demand forecasts are usually right-skewed, a long tail of what if it takes off scenarios.

216
00:18:24.329 --> 00:18:32.351
When the cost function is non-linear or has different slopes for different options, plugging in the most likely value can pick the wrong option.

217
00:18:32.631 --> 00:18:38.604
L4 shows exactly this: the point estimate chose serverless; the distribution chose VMs.

218
00:18:38.884 --> 00:18:41.359
Prefer options that keep options open.

219
00:18:41.639 --> 00:18:45.201
Under high uncertainty, the value of reversibility rises.

220
00:18:45.481 --> 00:18:50.985
A slightly more expensive choice that is easy to change can beat a cheaper choice that locks you in.

221
00:18:51.265 --> 00:18:58.455
This is real-options thinking: flexibility has a quantifiable value, and it is highest when uncertainty is highest.

222
00:18:59.805 --> 00:19:20.583
Related practices: last responsible moment (defer irreversible decisions until you have more information, but not so late that you've lost options by default), and decision triggers (commit now, but state the observable condition that would make you switch, L4's migrate at ~147M requests per month).

223
00:19:21.933 --> 00:19:25.112
Governance: fitness functions, reviews, and who decides.

224
00:19:25.392 --> 00:19:27.653
A decision that isn't enforced decays.

225
00:19:27.933 --> 00:19:29.768
Two complementary mechanisms:

226
00:19:30.048 --> 00:19:41.824
Fitness functions (from Building Evolutionary Architectures, Ford or Parsons or Kua) are automated checks that measure how well the system satisfies an architectural characteristic.

227
00:19:42.104 --> 00:19:45.112
They turn a whiteboard rule into a CI gate (L2):

228
00:19:46.462 --> 00:19:47.806
Here is a quick table:

229
00:19:48.086 --> 00:19:49.899
Kind, and Example, and Tooling.

230
00:19:50.179 --> 00:19:52.099
Structural (atomic, static).

231
00:19:52.379 --> 00:19:53.254
Example:

232
00:19:53.534 --> 00:19:57.331
Domain must not depend on infrastructure; no module cycles.

233
00:19:57.611 --> 00:19:58.422
Tooling:

234
00:19:58.702 --> 00:20:01.731
ArchUnit, jQAssistant, dependency-cruiser.

235
00:20:02.011 --> 00:20:03.355
Performance (triggered).

236
00:20:03.635 --> 00:20:04.510
Example:

237
00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:09.057
Checkout p99 300 ms under the load-test profile.

238
00:20:09.337 --> 00:20:10.147
Tooling:

239
00:20:10.427 --> 00:20:14.033
Gatling or k6 in CI, compared against a budget.

240
00:20:14.313 --> 00:20:16.083
Operational (continuous).

241
00:20:16.363 --> 00:20:17.238
Example:

242
00:20:17.518 --> 00:20:19.801
Error-budget burn rate below threshold.

243
00:20:20.081 --> 00:20:20.891
Tooling:

244
00:20:21.171 --> 00:20:23.667
SLO monitoring (Ch 24).

245
00:20:23.947 --> 00:20:24.886
Security.

246
00:20:25.166 --> 00:20:26.041
Example:

247
00:20:26.321 --> 00:20:30.289
No critical CVEs in dependencies; no secrets in code.

248
00:20:30.569 --> 00:20:31.379
Tooling:

249
00:20:31.659 --> 00:20:33.451
SCA or secret scanners.

250
00:20:33.731 --> 00:20:34.627
Holistic.

251
00:20:34.907 --> 00:20:35.782
Example:

252
00:20:36.062 --> 00:20:40.841
Chaos experiment: losing recommendations doesn't breach checkout SLO.

253
00:20:41.121 --> 00:20:41.931
Tooling:

254
00:20:42.211 --> 00:20:44.110
Ch 27 game days.

255
00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.361
Review processes handle what can't be automated.

256
00:20:48.641 --> 00:20:57.729
The failure mode to avoid is the heavyweight Architecture Review Board that every change must queue for, it becomes a bottleneck and teams route around it.

257
00:20:58.009 --> 00:20:59.033
Better patterns:

258
00:20:59.313 --> 00:21:10.129
Architecture advice process, anyone can make an architectural decision, but they must seek advice from those affected and from people with expertise, and record it in an ADR.

259
00:21:10.409 --> 00:21:12.095
The decider stays accountable.

260
00:21:13.445 --> 00:21:27.034
Tiered review, two-way-door decisions are made by teams; one-way-door or cross-cutting decisions (a new datastore technology, a new integration style, anything touching the security boundary) get a lightweight review.

261
00:21:27.314 --> 00:21:37.341
Paved road or golden path, make the recommended choice the easiest choice (templates, platform defaults), so governance happens by default rather than by gate.

262
00:21:37.621 --> 00:21:48.202
Technology radar, publish Adopt or Trial or Assess or Hold for technologies, so teams know which choices need no justification and which need an ADR.

263
00:21:49.552 --> 00:21:52.048
Cognitive traps in architecture decisions.

264
00:21:52.328 --> 00:21:55.293
Architects are human and the same biases recur:

265
00:21:55.573 --> 00:22:01.888
Résumé-driven development, choosing technology because it's interesting to learn, not because it fits.

266
00:22:02.168 --> 00:22:06.093
Counter: the team-fit and operability criteria in the matrix.

267
00:22:06.373 --> 00:22:09.787
Hype or cargo cult, Netflix does microservices.

268
00:22:10.067 --> 00:22:12.413
Netflix has problems of a scale you don't.

269
00:22:12.693 --> 00:22:14.421
Counter: context drivers.

270
00:22:14.701 --> 00:22:18.691
Sunk cost, keeping a decision because of the effort already invested.

271
00:22:18.971 --> 00:22:22.107
Counter: revisit triggers evaluated on current facts.

272
00:22:23.457 --> 00:22:28.641
Anchoring, the first option presented becomes the reference everything else is compared to.

273
00:22:28.921 --> 00:22:32.398
Counter: generate options independently before discussing.

274
00:22:32.678 --> 00:22:39.803
Confirmation bias or HiPPO, weighting criteria to favor the option the highest-paid person prefers.

275
00:22:40.083 --> 00:22:44.969
Counter: weights before scores; independent scoring; sensitivity analysis.

276
00:22:45.249 --> 00:22:50.774
Analysis paralysis, refusing to decide until uncertainty is gone (it never goes).

277
00:22:51.054 --> 00:22:55.747
Counter: two-way doors decided fast; time-boxed spikes; decision triggers.

278
00:22:57.097 --> 00:22:59.188
Point-estimate fallacy, L4.

279
00:23:00.538 --> 00:23:02.287
Technical debt as a decision.

280
00:23:02.567 --> 00:23:15.154
Technical debt is best understood as a decision to trade future cost for present speed, and like financial debt, it's fine when taken deliberately and serviced, disastrous when accumulated accidentally.

281
00:23:15.434 --> 00:23:31.967
Martin Fowler's quadrant separates deliberate versus inadvertent and prudent versus reckless: we must ship now and will clean up after launch (deliberate, prudent) is a legitimate architectural decision and deserves an ADR with a repayment trigger; what's layering?

282
00:23:32.247 --> 00:23:37.197
(inadvertent, reckless) is erosion that fitness functions exist to catch (L2).

283
00:23:38.547 --> 00:23:51.219
Making debt a first-class decision means recording the shortcut, its expected cost (interest), and its repayment trigger, and tracking it like any other backlog item rather than letting it silently compound.

284
00:23:52.569 --> 00:23:53.465
