Architects argue sync versus. async with adjectives; here we DECIDE it with numbers.

444
00:37:06.691 --> 00:37:09.869
Checkout needs data from 4 downstream services.

445
00:37:10.149 --> 00:37:11.216
We compare two.

446
00:37:14.066 --> 00:37:14.983
On screen:

447
00:37:15.263 --> 00:37:22.538
Decision: which checkout design meets a 300 ms p99 SLO at 800 req per second?

448
00:37:22.818 --> 00:37:48.866
Four downstream. services. service svc(ms) cap(r per second) ρ p99(ms). catalog 20 2000 0.40 81.3. inventory 35 2500 0.32 110.8. pricing 15 3000 0.27 40.1. promotions 25 2000 0.40 101.7.

449
00:37:49.146 --> 00:37:56.762
A) SEQUENTIAL end-to-end p99 = sum = 333.9 ms, then BREACHES SLO.

450
00:37:57.042 --> 00:38:04.274
B) PARALLEL end-to-end p99 = max = 110.8 ms, then MEETS SLO.

451
00:38:04.554 --> 00:38:05.471
Bottleneck:

452
00:38:05.751 --> 00:38:12.450
'catalog' (capacity 2000 r per second), headroom at 800 r per second = 60%.

453
00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:13.605
Beyond.

454
00:38:13.885 --> 00:38:15.399
The rest is in the chapter.

455
00:38:18.249 --> 00:38:19.103
Reading it:

456
00:38:19.383 --> 00:38:20.321
Sequential:

457
00:38:20.601 --> 00:38:29.711
334 ms, breaches the 300 ms SLO, even though no single service is slow; the budget is spent by addition.

458
00:38:29.991 --> 00:38:30.844
Parallel:

459
00:38:31.124 --> 00:38:37.055
111 ms, meets it, because end-to-end latency is the slowest call, not the sum.

460
00:38:37.335 --> 00:38:41.004
The debate sync chain versus fan-out is settled by arithmetic.

461
00:38:41.284 --> 00:38:46.383
Every service's p99 is well above its raw service time (inventory:

462
00:38:46.663 --> 00:38:55.793
35 ms, then 111 ms at ρ = 0.32), queueing delay is part of latency even at modest load.

463
00:38:57.143 --> 00:39:13.719
The cliff: the same 35 ms service goes from 196 ms at ρ = 0.5 to 679 ms at 0.8, 1.5 s at 0.9, and 16 s at 0.99.

464
00:39:13.999 --> 00:39:28.506
No design survives a saturated dependency, so the second architectural decision is capacity headroom (target utilization ~50 to 70%, autoscaling on it, Ch 25, Ch 26).

465
00:39:28.786 --> 00:39:39.154
The M or M or 1 model is an approximation (real services aren't exponential); its job is to compare designs and locate cliffs before building.

466
00:39:39.434 --> 00:39:43.423
A load test (a performance fitness function) verifies afterward.

467
00:39:44.773 --> 00:39:46.949
L4, Decision under uncertainty:

468
00:39:47.229 --> 00:39:48.317
Monte Carlo.

469
00:39:48.597 --> 00:39:49.536
Requirement:

470
00:39:49.816 --> 00:39:52.589
Serverless or our own VMs for the new service?

471
00:39:52.869 --> 00:39:57.968
Traffic next year could be anywhere from 40M to 400M requests a month.

472
00:39:58.248 --> 00:40:01.576
Serverless costs a fixed price per request (no ceiling).

473
00:40:01.856 --> 00:40:08.853
VMs cost a fleet sized for peak, with a 6-VM multi-AZ floor and a much lower marginal cost.

474
00:40:09.133 --> 00:40:16.579
Demand is a right-skewed triangular distribution (40M or 120M likely or 400M).

475
00:40:16.859 --> 00:40:21.723
We compare the naive point estimate with 200,000 Monte Carlo trials.

476
00:40:23.073 --> 00:40:26.401
Here is the Java code, MonteCarloL4.java.

477
00:40:26.681 --> 00:40:30.755
It runs to 118 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

478
00:40:31.035 --> 00:40:39.355
MonteCarloL4.java, run with: java MonteCarloL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

479
00:40:39.635 --> 00:40:41.491
A DECISION UNDER UNCERTAINTY.

480
00:40:41.771 --> 00:40:45.291
We must choose the compute model for a new service for the next year:

481
00:40:45.571 --> 00:40:47.491
A) SERVERLESS, pay per request.

482
00:40:47.771 --> 00:40:52.059
Cheap at low volume, but cost grows linearly with usage (no ceiling).

483
00:40:54.909 --> 00:40:55.827
On screen:

484
00:40:56.107 --> 00:40:57.749
Decision under uncertainty:

485
00:40:58.029 --> 00:41:00.739
Serverless versus owned VMs for next year.

486
00:41:01.019 --> 00:41:02.341
Monthly volume is.

487
00:41:02.621 --> 00:41:03.581
UNCERTAIN.

488
00:41:03.861 --> 00:41:11.093
(triangular: pessimistic 40M, likely 120M, optimistic 400M requests per month).

489
00:41:11.373 --> 00:41:21.912
Naive point estimate at the 'likely' 120M per month: serverless $23,040 versus VMs $28,080, then.

490
00:41:22.192 --> 00:41:23.387
SERVERLESS cheaper.

491
00:41:23.667 --> 00:41:48.563
Monte Carlo (200,000 trials over the uncertain volume): option mean $ p10 $ p90 $. serverless $35,895 $18,051 $57,582. owned VMs $29,594 $28,080 $32,760.

492
00:41:48.843 --> 00:42:03.477
P(serverless cheaper) = 36% P(VMs cheaper) = 64%. break-even volume about 147M requests per month (below, then serverless wins, above, then VMs.

493
00:42:03.757 --> 00:42:05.272
The rest is in the chapter.

494
00:42:08.122 --> 00:42:08.975
Reading it:

495
00:42:09.255 --> 00:42:12.114
The point estimate and the distribution disagree.

496
00:42:12.394 --> 00:42:19.519
At the likely 120M per month, serverless looks cheaper ($23k versus $28k).

497
00:42:19.799 --> 00:42:30.957
Across all plausible futures, VMs are cheaper in expectation ($29.6k versus $35.9k) and in 64% of trials.

498
00:42:32.307 --> 00:42:43.037
Why: the demand distribution is right-skewed, the mean (~187M) is well above the mode (120M), and the options have different slopes.

499
00:42:43.317 --> 00:42:57.355
Serverless is cheaper below the ~147M break-even and increasingly expensive above it, with no ceiling (p90 $57.6k versus VMs' $32.8k).

500
00:42:57.635 --> 00:43:01.795
Plugging in the mode ignored exactly the region where the decision flips.

501
00:43:02.075 --> 00:43:15.920
The honest ADR offers either VMs or serverless now with a pre-agreed migration trigger at ~147M per month, and, for the second, designs the functions to keep the exit cost low.

502
00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:20.253
The single-number comparison is the one option that isn't defensible.

503
00:43:21.603 --> 00:43:31.203
This generalizes to every decision with uncertain inputs and non-linear or differently-sloped outcomes: capacity, cost, vendor pricing, headcount.

504
00:43:32.553 --> 00:43:33.492
Part 6.

505
00:43:33.772 --> 00:43:35.052
Production Concerns.

506
00:43:36.302 --> 00:43:39.139
Failure modes of architectural decision-making.

507
00:43:39.419 --> 00:43:40.763
Here is a quick table:

508
00:43:41.043 --> 00:43:43.326
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

509
00:43:43.606 --> 00:43:45.099
Undocumented decisions.

510
00:43:45.379 --> 00:43:46.190
Symptom:

511
00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:51.099
Nobody can explain why (§1.1); re-litigated repeatedly.

512
00:43:51.379 --> 00:43:52.382
Mitigation:

513
00:43:52.662 --> 00:43:55.414
ADRs in repo with rejected options.

514
00:43:55.694 --> 00:43:56.974
Fragile decisions.

515
00:43:57.254 --> 00:43:58.065
Symptom:

516
00:43:58.345 --> 00:44:01.267
Choice flips when someone disagrees slightly on a weight.

517
00:44:01.547 --> 00:44:02.550
Mitigation:

518
00:44:02.830 --> 00:44:05.774
Sensitivity analysis before committing (L1).

519
00:44:06.054 --> 00:44:07.569
Adjective-driven design.

520
00:44:07.849 --> 00:44:08.659
Symptom:

521
00:44:08.939 --> 00:44:12.438
Scalable, fast with no numbers; SLO breached in prod.

522
00:44:12.718 --> 00:44:13.721
Mitigation:

523
00:44:14.001 --> 00:44:16.113
Quantitative validation (L3).

524
00:44:16.393 --> 00:44:17.843
Point-estimate decisions.

525
00:44:18.123 --> 00:44:18.934
Symptom:

526
00:44:19.214 --> 00:44:22.371
Cost overruns when demand deviates from the forecast.

527
00:44:22.651 --> 00:44:23.654
Mitigation:

528
00:44:23.934 --> 00:44:27.881
Distributions + Monte Carlo + break-even (L4).

529
00:44:28.161 --> 00:44:29.590
Architecture erosion.

530
00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:30.681
Symptom:

531
00:44:30.961 --> 00:44:34.011
Diagram says layered; code says big ball of mud.

532
00:44:34.291 --> 00:44:35.294
Mitigation:

533
00:44:35.574 --> 00:44:37.771
Fitness functions in CI (L2).

534
00:44:39.121 --> 00:44:40.935
Decisions never revisited.

535
00:44:41.215 --> 00:44:42.025
Symptom:

536
00:44:42.305 --> 00:44:45.953
Drivers changed, decision stands (§1.1).

537
00:44:46.233 --> 00:44:47.236
Mitigation:

538
00:44:47.516 --> 00:44:49.585
Revisit triggers + quarterly review.

539
00:44:49.865 --> 00:44:51.209
Governance bottleneck.

540
00:44:51.489 --> 00:44:52.300
Symptom:

541
00:44:52.580 --> 00:44:54.863
ARB queue; teams route around it.

542
00:44:55.143 --> 00:44:56.145
Mitigation:

543
00:44:56.425 --> 00:44:59.177
Advice process; tiered review; paved roads.

544
00:44:59.457 --> 00:45:00.887
Analysis paralysis.

545
00:45:01.167 --> 00:45:01.977
Symptom:

546
00:45:02.257 --> 00:45:03.751
Two-way doors take weeks.

547
00:45:04.031 --> 00:45:05.033
Mitigation:

548
00:45:05.313 --> 00:45:08.684
Decide reversible choices fast; time-box spikes.

549
00:45:10.034 --> 00:45:12.914
Observability of decisions (Ch 24).

550
00:45:13.194 --> 00:45:26.293
Revisit triggers should be metrics you already collect, req per second, p99, cost per month, deploy frequency, change-coupling between modules, so the quarterly review is a dashboard, not an investigation.

551
00:45:26.573 --> 00:45:33.719
Track fitness-function results over time: the count of suppressed violations is a leading indicator of erosion.

552
00:45:33.999 --> 00:45:49.999
For decisions made under uncertainty, track the actual value of the uncertain input (for example, monthly request volume) against the distribution you assumed; when reality exits your pessimistic or optimistic range, the decision needs revisiting.

553
00:45:51.349 --> 00:45:52.288
Security.

554
00:45:52.568 --> 00:46:04.557
Treat the security boundary as a one-way door: new trust boundaries, new data stores holding PII, new third-party integrations always get a reviewed ADR (Ch 23).

555
00:46:04.837 --> 00:46:13.563
Add security fitness functions: dependency CVE thresholds, no secrets in code, authz annotations required on controllers.

556
00:46:13.843 --> 00:46:22.888
ADRs themselves may contain sensitive reasoning (threat models, vendor contracts), keep those in an access-controlled repo or redact.

557
00:46:24.238 --> 00:46:26.286
Performance of the decision process.

558
00:46:26.566 --> 00:46:35.398
Time-box: a two-way door should be decided in hours; a significant ADR in days; an ATAM-style evaluation in a week or two.

559
00:46:35.678 --> 00:46:43.913
Use spikes to replace the most uncertain score with evidence, a two-day prototype is cheap compared with a wrong one-way-door decision.

560
00:46:44.193 --> 00:46:49.611
Reuse: a published technology radar and decision templates remove repeated debates.

561
00:46:50.961 --> 00:46:51.687
Cost.

562
00:46:51.967 --> 00:47:00.841
The cost of a decision includes total cost of ownership: build, run, operate, train, hire, and exit (the cost of reversing it later).

563
00:47:01.121 --> 00:47:04.919
Exit cost is what makes a door one-way; estimate it explicitly.

564
00:47:05.199 --> 00:47:06.841
Weight operability honestly.

565
00:47:07.121 --> 00:47:19.857
The §1.1 platform's 26 services cost roughly 30% of engineering capacity in overhead, a cost that never appeared in the original decision because it wasn't a criterion.

566
00:47:21.207 --> 00:47:22.146
Part 7.

567
00:47:22.426 --> 00:47:24.133
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

568
00:47:25.383 --> 00:47:26.300
Watch out:

569
00:47:26.580 --> 00:47:27.220
1.

570
00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:29.164
The undocumented decision.

571
00:47:29.444 --> 00:47:35.353
A significant, expensive-to-reverse decision with no record of context, options or trade-offs.

572
00:47:35.633 --> 00:47:40.775
It will be misunderstood, re-litigated or blindly preserved (§1.1).

573
00:47:41.055 --> 00:47:42.207
Write the ADR.

574
00:47:43.557 --> 00:47:44.474
Watch out:

575
00:47:44.754 --> 00:47:45.458
2.

576
00:47:45.738 --> 00:47:47.807
One option presented as a decision.

577
00:47:48.087 --> 00:47:52.653
If only one option was considered, it's a rationalization, not a decision.

578
00:47:52.933 --> 00:47:56.026
Always include at least three, including the status quo.

579
00:47:57.376 --> 00:47:58.293
Watch out:

580
00:47:58.573 --> 00:47:59.320
3.

581
00:47:59.600 --> 00:48:01.307
Weights chosen after scores.

582
00:48:01.587 --> 00:48:05.533
Adjusting weights once you've seen the scores lets you crown any favorite.

583
00:48:05.813 --> 00:48:08.181
Agree weights first; score independently.

584
00:48:09.531 --> 00:48:10.449
Watch out:

585
00:48:10.729 --> 00:48:11.433
4.

586
00:48:11.713 --> 00:48:13.462
No sensitivity analysis.

587
00:48:13.742 --> 00:48:18.414
A matrix without sensitivity hides whether the winner is robust or a coin-flip.

588
00:48:18.694 --> 00:48:21.382
Always ask which weight flips it, and how far?

589
00:48:21.662 --> 00:48:22.494
(L1).

590
00:48:23.844 --> 00:48:24.761
Watch out:

591
00:48:25.041 --> 00:48:25.809
5.

592
00:48:26.089 --> 00:48:27.903
Scoring what you could calculate.

593
00:48:28.183 --> 00:48:29.313
Giving performance:

594
00:48:29.593 --> 00:48:33.092
4 or 5 to a design whose latency you could have modeled.

595
00:48:33.372 --> 00:48:34.716
Compute it (L3).

596
00:48:36.066 --> 00:48:36.983
Watch out:

597
00:48:37.263 --> 00:48:38.010
6.

598
00:48:38.290 --> 00:48:39.805
The single-number forecast.

599
00:48:40.085 --> 00:48:43.327
Choosing based on the most likely value of a skewed input.

600
00:48:43.607 --> 00:48:46.594
The distribution can reverse the decision (L4).

601
00:48:47.944 --> 00:48:48.861
Watch out:

602
00:48:49.141 --> 00:48:49.888
7.

603
00:48:50.168 --> 00:48:51.939
Diagram-only architecture.

604
00:48:52.219 --> 00:48:55.931
Rules that live only on a whiteboard erode with every PR.

605
00:48:56.211 --> 00:48:58.344
Make them fitness functions (L2).

606
00:48:59.694 --> 00:49:00.611
Watch out:

607
00:49:00.891 --> 00:49:01.574
8.

608
00:49:01.854 --> 00:49:03.689
Editing accepted ADRs.

609
00:49:03.969 --> 00:49:07.595
Rewriting history destroys the record of why things changed.

610
00:49:07.875 --> 00:49:09.795
Supersede with a new ADR.

611
00:49:11.145 --> 00:49:12.063
Watch out:

612
00:49:12.343 --> 00:49:13.089
9.

613
00:49:13.369 --> 00:49:14.649
No revisit trigger.

614
00:49:14.929 --> 00:49:21.820
Decisions without a reopen condition either never get revisited (sunk cost) or get re-debated constantly.

615
00:49:22.100 --> 00:49:23.807
State the observable trigger.

616
00:49:25.157 --> 00:49:26.074
Watch out:

617
00:49:26.354 --> 00:49:27.058
10.

618
00:49:27.338 --> 00:49:29.898
Treating every decision as a one-way door.

619
00:49:30.178 --> 00:49:36.173
Heavy review for reversible choices creates bottlenecks and teaches teams to bypass governance.

620
00:49:36.453 --> 00:49:38.202
Fast lane for two-way doors.

621
00:49:39.552 --> 00:49:40.469
Watch out:

622
00:49:40.749 --> 00:49:41.581
11.

623
00:49:41.861 --> 00:49:44.315
Résumé-driven or hype-driven choices.

624
00:49:44.595 --> 00:49:48.435
Everyone uses Kubernetes or Kafka or microservices.

625
00:49:48.715 --> 00:49:54.539
Your context, scale and team decide, make team-fit and operability explicit criteria.

626
00:49:55.889 --> 00:49:56.806
Watch out:

627
00:49:57.086 --> 00:49:57.875
12.

628
00:49:58.155 --> 00:49:59.841
The Ivory-tower architect.

629
00:50:00.121 --> 00:50:04.473
Decisions made far from the code, by people who won't operate the result.

630
00:50:04.753 --> 00:50:09.681
Use the advice process: the people accountable for running it decide, with advice.

631
00:50:11.031 --> 00:50:11.905
Part 8.

632
00:50:12.185 --> 00:50:13.529
Interview Q and A.

633
00:50:14.779 --> 00:50:15.611
Beginner.

634
00:50:15.891 --> 00:50:16.915
Question 1.

635
00:50:17.195 --> 00:50:20.374
What is an Architecture Decision Record, and why write one?

636
00:50:20.654 --> 00:50:22.019
Thirty-second answer:

637
00:50:22.299 --> 00:50:38.001
An ADR is a short document recording one significant architectural decision: the context and drivers, the decision, the options considered and why they were rejected, the consequences (good and bad), and ideally what would make you revisit it.

638
00:50:38.281 --> 00:50:54.473
You write it because architectural decisions are expensive to reverse and outlive the people who made them, without a record, future engineers either blindly preserve a decision whose reasons no longer hold or re-litigate it without knowing what was already evaluated.

639
00:50:55.823 --> 00:50:56.825
Question 2.

640
00:50:57.105 --> 00:51:00.497
What are quality attributes and why do they drive architecture?

641
00:51:00.777 --> 00:51:02.143
Thirty-second answer:

642
00:51:02.423 --> 00:51:12.215
Quality attributes are the non-functional properties, performance, scalability, availability, security, modifiability, operability, cost, time-to-market.

643
00:51:12.495 --> 00:51:29.412
They drive architecture more than features do: two systems with identical features can need completely different architectures if one serves 10 req per second at 99.5% and the other 10,000 req per second at 99.99%.

644
00:51:29.692 --> 00:51:34.791
They also conflict, so prioritizing them is the core of architectural trade-offs.

645
00:51:36.141 --> 00:51:37.186
Question 3.

646
00:51:37.466 --> 00:51:40.687
What is a one-way door versus a two-way door decision?

647
00:51:40.967 --> 00:51:42.333
Thirty-second answer:

648
00:51:42.613 --> 00:51:53.834
A one-way door is a decision that's very expensive or impossible to reverse, choosing a primary database, a data model shared across teams, a public API contract.

649
00:51:54.114 --> 00:52:01.239
A two-way door is cheap to reverse, a library choice behind an interface, a config value, an internal endpoint.

650
00:52:01.519 --> 00:52:09.178
One-way doors deserve analysis, options and an ADR; two-way doors should be made quickly by the team closest to the work.

651
00:52:10.528 --> 00:52:11.595
Question 4.

652
00:52:11.875 --> 00:52:14.115
Why should you consider at least three options?

653
00:52:14.395 --> 00:52:15.760
Thirty-second answer:

654
00:52:16.040 --> 00:52:21.864
Because with one option you're rationalizing, and with two you're in a false binary that hides alternatives.

655
00:52:22.144 --> 00:52:32.640
Three or more, always including keep what we have, forces you to articulate criteria, surfaces hybrid solutions, and gives the decision matrix something meaningful to compare.

656
00:52:32.920 --> 00:52:38.573
The status quo is especially important because change has costs that the new option must beat.

657
00:52:39.923 --> 00:52:40.926
Intermediate.

658
00:52:41.206 --> 00:52:42.315
Question 5.

659
00:52:42.595 --> 00:52:46.286
How do you run a weighted decision matrix without it becoming theatre?

660
00:52:46.566 --> 00:52:47.931
Thirty-second answer:

661
00:52:48.211 --> 00:53:02.483
Agree criteria and weights before anyone scores; have several people score independently; discuss divergent scores (that's where facts are missing); calculate; then run a sensitivity analysis to see how robust the winner is.

662
00:53:02.763 --> 00:53:11.702
Treat the matrix as a tool for making reasoning visible and arguable, not as an oracle, and replace any score you could calculate with the calculation.

663
00:53:13.052 --> 00:53:14.161
Question 6.

664
00:53:14.441 --> 00:53:17.385
What is sensitivity analysis and what does it tell you?

665
00:53:17.665 --> 00:53:19.031
Thirty-second answer:

666
00:53:19.311 --> 00:53:24.793
You vary the inputs, usually the weights, sometimes the scores, and see whether the winner changes.

667
00:53:25.073 --> 00:53:31.580
If a small, plausible change flips it, the decision is fragile and you need more information before committing.

668
00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:35.551
If only an extreme change flips it, the decision is robust.

669
00:53:35.831 --> 00:53:41.527
It also tells you which assumptions actually matter, so you know what to monitor as a revisit trigger.

670
00:53:42.877 --> 00:53:43.965
Question 7.

671
00:53:44.245 --> 00:53:46.293
What is an architecture fitness function?

672
00:53:46.573 --> 00:53:47.938
Thirty-second answer:

673
00:53:48.218 --> 00:54:05.050
An automated check that measures whether the system still satisfies an architectural characteristic, for example the domain layer must not depend on infrastructure, no dependency cycles between modules, or checkout p99 300 ms under the load profile.

674
00:54:05.330 --> 00:54:12.733
Running them in CI means architectural rules are enforced on every change instead of living only on a diagram.

675
00:54:14.083 --> 00:54:15.107
Question 8.

676
00:54:15.387 --> 00:54:18.011
How do you make a performance requirement testable?

677
00:54:18.291 --> 00:54:19.656
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:54:19.936 --> 00:54:26.891
Use a quality-attribute scenario: source, stimulus, artifact, environment, response, and a response measure.

679
00:54:27.171 --> 00:54:37.432
Under 800 req per second of peak checkout traffic, the checkout API responds with p99 300 ms and 0.1% errors.

680
00:54:37.712 --> 00:54:44.752
Then you can model it before building (L3) and verify it in a load test afterward (a performance fitness function).

681
00:54:46.102 --> 00:54:47.169
Question 9.

682
00:54:47.449 --> 00:54:50.627
How does technical debt relate to architecture decisions?

683
00:54:50.907 --> 00:54:52.273
Thirty-second answer:

684
00:54:52.553 --> 00:55:04.009
Deliberate technical debt is an architecture decision, trading future cost for present speed, and deserves the same treatment: record it, estimate its interest, and set a repayment trigger.

685
00:55:04.289 --> 00:55:08.897
Inadvertent debt is architecture erosion, which fitness functions help catch.

686
00:55:09.177 --> 00:55:14.233
The problem isn't debt; it's unrecorded, unserviced debt that compounds silently.

687
00:55:15.583 --> 00:55:16.351
Senior.

688
00:55:16.631 --> 00:55:17.676
Question 10.

689
00:55:17.956 --> 00:55:22.287
Monolith, modular monolith, or microservices, how would you decide?

690
00:55:22.567 --> 00:55:23.932
Thirty-second answer:

691
00:55:24.212 --> 00:55:25.791
By drivers, not fashion.

692
00:55:26.071 --> 00:55:29.377
Key questions: how many teams need independent deploys?

693
00:55:29.657 --> 00:55:33.903
Do components have genuinely different scaling or availability needs?

694
00:55:34.183 --> 00:55:39.388
How much operational maturity (observability, CI or CD, on-call) exists?

695
00:55:39.668 --> 00:55:41.033
What's the business stage?

696
00:55:41.313 --> 00:55:53.196
For most organizations under ~50 engineers without divergent scaling needs, a modular monolith with enforced boundaries wins; extract services where a real driver exists.

697
00:55:53.476 --> 00:55:57.935
I'd make this a weighted matrix with sensitivity, and state the revisit trigger.

698
00:55:59.285 --> 00:56:00.415
Question 11.

699
00:56:00.695 --> 00:56:03.810
How do you use queueing theory in architecture decisions?

700
00:56:04.090 --> 00:56:05.455
Thirty-second answer:

701
00:56:05.735 --> 00:56:08.722
To predict latency and capacity before building.

702
00:56:09.002 --> 00:56:09.919
Key facts:

703
00:56:10.199 --> 00:56:25.175
Little's Law (concurrency = throughput times latency), and M or M or 1 response time R = S/(1minus ρ), latency grows non-linearly with utilization and goes vertical near 100%.

704
00:56:25.455 --> 00:56:37.786
Practically: sequential calls add latencies, parallel calls take the max, and each service needs headroom (target ~50 to 70% utilization) or its tail latency explodes.

705
00:56:39.136 --> 00:56:40.224
Question 12.

706
00:56:40.504 --> 00:56:44.088
How do you decide when inputs like future traffic are uncertain?

707
00:56:44.368 --> 00:56:45.733
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:56:46.013 --> 00:56:56.211
Estimate ranges (pessimistic or likely or optimistic), model them as distributions, and run a Monte Carlo over the decision's cost or performance model.

709
00:56:56.491 --> 00:57:03.403
Report expected value, spread (p10 or p90), probability each option wins, and break-even points.

710
00:57:03.683 --> 00:57:10.595
Prefer reversible options when uncertainty is high, and set a decision trigger tied to the uncertain variable.

711
00:57:11.945 --> 00:57:13.225
Question 13.

712
00:57:13.505 --> 00:57:17.729
How do you govern architecture across many teams without becoming a bottleneck?

713
00:57:18.009 --> 00:57:19.374
Thirty-second answer:

714
00:57:19.654 --> 00:57:34.353
Replace gates with an advice process and automation: teams make decisions (two-way doors freely), seek advice from affected teams and experts for significant ones, and record ADRs; one-way doors get a lightweight review.

715
00:57:34.633 --> 00:57:46.409
Enforce what can be automated with fitness functions, make the recommended path the easiest via paved roads and templates, and publish a technology radar so common choices need no debate.

716
00:57:47.759 --> 00:57:48.953
Question 14.

717
00:57:49.233 --> 00:57:50.961
What makes a good revisit trigger?

718
00:57:51.241 --> 00:57:52.607
Thirty-second answer:

719
00:57:52.887 --> 00:57:59.927
It's observable, specific, and tied to the assumption the decision depends on, ideally a metric you already collect.

720
00:58:00.207 --> 00:58:16.079
Revisit when sustained load exceeds 1,500 req per second, when monthly volume passes 147M requests, when a module needs an independent release cadence, when the scalability weight exceeds ~8.75.

721
00:58:16.359 --> 00:58:19.153
Vague triggers like when it stops scaling are useless.

722
00:58:20.503 --> 00:58:21.399
Architect.

723
00:58:21.679 --> 00:58:22.874
Question 15.

724
00:58:23.154 --> 00:58:27.165
You inherit a system whose major decisions were never documented.

725
00:58:27.445 --> 00:58:28.447
What do you do?

726
00:58:28.727 --> 00:58:30.093
Thirty-second answer:

727
00:58:30.373 --> 00:58:32.357
Reconstruct before redesigning.

728
00:58:32.637 --> 00:58:46.525
For each major decision, recover the context (interview people, read history, look at the metrics of the time), list the options that plausibly existed, infer the drivers, and write retroactive ADRs marked as such.

729
00:58:46.805 --> 00:58:51.626
Then check each against current drivers, often you'll find the reasons no longer hold.

730
00:58:51.906 --> 00:58:57.922
Only then propose changes, as new ADRs with options, sensitivity and revisit triggers.

731
00:58:59.272 --> 00:59:00.467
Question 16.

732
00:59:00.747 --> 00:59:04.117
Explain sensitivity points and trade-off points (ATAM).

733
00:59:04.397 --> 00:59:05.763
Thirty-second answer:

734
00:59:06.043 --> 00:59:15.920
A sensitivity point is an architectural decision that strongly affects one quality attribute, for example, a connection-pool size that governs tail latency.

735
00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:28.957
A trade-off point is a decision that affects several attributes in opposite directions, for example, synchronous replication improves durability (RPO 0) but hurts latency and availability.

736
00:59:29.237 --> 00:59:34.165
Trade-off points are where architects earn their keep, because every choice there costs something.

737
00:59:35.515 --> 00:59:36.859
Question 17.

738
00:59:37.139 --> 00:59:40.382
How do you estimate and weigh the exit cost of a decision?

739
00:59:40.662 --> 00:59:42.027
Thirty-second answer:

740
00:59:42.307 --> 00:59:51.331
Estimate what it would cost to reverse: data migration, rewriting integrations, retraining, contract penalties, dual-running during transition.

741
00:59:51.611 --> 00:59:55.089
High exit cost is what makes a decision a one-way door.

742
00:59:55.369 --> 01:00:06.291
Under uncertainty, weight reversibility highly, a slightly more expensive but reversible choice can dominate, because flexibility has option value that grows with uncertainty.

743
01:00:07.641 --> 01:00:08.815
Question 18.

744
01:00:09.095 --> 01:00:13.681
How do you handle a strong disagreement between two senior engineers on a decision?

745
01:00:13.961 --> 01:00:15.327
Thirty-second answer:

746
01:00:15.607 --> 01:00:17.292
Make the disagreement precise.

747
01:00:17.572 --> 01:00:26.212
Put both options in a matrix; find whether they differ on weights (a values or priority disagreement) or scores (a factual disagreement).

748
01:00:26.492 --> 01:00:30.567
Factual disagreements get evidence, a spike, a model, a measurement.

749
01:00:30.847 --> 01:00:35.263
Priority disagreements get escalated to whoever owns the business priority.

750
01:00:35.543 --> 01:00:41.943
Run sensitivity: if the decision is robust across both people's weights, the disagreement doesn't matter.

751
01:00:42.223 --> 01:00:44.292
Record the dissent in the ADR.

752
01:00:45.642 --> 01:00:46.879
Question 19.

753
01:00:47.159 --> 01:00:50.530
What fitness functions would you put on a modular monolith?

754
01:00:50.810 --> 01:00:52.175
Thirty-second answer:

755
01:00:52.455 --> 01:01:06.855
Structural: module boundaries (modules talk only through published APIs), no cycles between modules, domain purity (no framework or IO dependencies in domain), no cross-module database table access.

756
01:01:07.135 --> 01:01:11.274
Operational: build time and test time budgets, deploy frequency.

757
01:01:11.554 --> 01:01:16.909
Performance: p99 latency budgets per critical endpoint under a load profile.

758
01:01:17.189 --> 01:01:24.762
Coupling metrics: change-coupling between modules (co-changed files) as an early warning of a boundary in the wrong place.

759
01:01:26.112 --> 01:01:27.221
Question 20.

760
01:01:27.501 --> 01:01:31.704
What distinguishes a senior engineer's decision-making from an architect's?

761
01:01:31.984 --> 01:01:33.349
Thirty-second answer:

762
01:01:33.629 --> 01:01:36.061
Scope, time horizon, and explicitness.

763
01:01:36.341 --> 01:01:42.891
A senior engineer makes excellent decisions within a component, mostly two-way doors, often intuitively.

764
01:01:43.171 --> 01:01:59.683
An architect makes and facilitates decisions across components and teams, for the long term, deliberately, framing drivers, surfacing options others didn't see, quantifying, recording, enforcing through fitness functions, and designing for reversibility.

765
01:01:59.963 --> 01:02:06.085
And increasingly, the architect's job is to help others decide well rather than to decide everything.

766
01:02:07.435 --> 01:02:08.331
Part 9.

767
01:02:08.611 --> 01:02:10.019
Summary and Practice.

768
01:02:11.269 --> 01:02:12.357
Key takeaways.

769
01:02:12.637 --> 01:02:16.541
Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to change.

770
01:02:16.821 --> 01:02:22.645
Distinguish one-way from two-way doors and apply process proportionately (§2.1).

771
01:02:22.925 --> 01:02:25.251
Quality attributes drive architecture.

772
01:02:25.531 --> 01:02:32.101
Make them measurable, prioritize them, and include constraints and business context (§2.2).

773
01:02:32.381 --> 01:02:34.963
Options, criteria, weights, then scores.

774
01:02:35.243 --> 01:02:42.048
At least three options including the status quo; weights before scores; independent scoring (§2.3).

775
01:02:42.328 --> 01:02:48.451
Sensitivity analysis tells you if a decision is robust and yields the revisit trigger (L1).

776
01:02:49.801 --> 01:02:57.715
Record decisions as ADRs, in the repo, immutable, with rejected options and a revisit trigger (§2.4).

777
01:02:57.995 --> 01:03:03.435
Enforce decisions with fitness functions so architecture can't silently erode (L2).

778
01:03:03.715 --> 01:03:05.379
Compute what you can compute.

779
01:03:05.659 --> 01:03:10.225
Latency budgets and queueing math settle arguments adjectives can't (L3).

780
01:03:10.505 --> 01:03:13.171
Decide under uncertainty with distributions.

781
01:03:13.451 --> 01:03:17.291
Skew and tails can reverse a point-estimate decision (L4).

782
01:03:18.641 --> 01:03:25.255
Govern with an advice process, paved roads and automation, not a bottleneck board (§2.8).

783
01:03:26.605 --> 01:03:27.927
Decision cheat sheet.

784
01:03:28.207 --> 01:03:29.551
Here is a quick table:

785
01:03:29.831 --> 01:03:31.495
Situation, and Approach.

786
01:03:31.775 --> 01:03:33.589
Reversible, local decision:

787
01:03:33.869 --> 01:03:36.578
Team decides quickly; no ADR needed.

788
01:03:36.858 --> 01:03:39.397
Expensive-to-reverse or cross-team decision:

789
01:03:39.677 --> 01:03:45.949
ADR with ≥3 options, weighted criteria, sensitivity, revisit trigger.

790
01:03:46.229 --> 01:03:47.487
Scores disagree:

791
01:03:47.767 --> 01:03:50.263
Facts: gather evidence (spike, model).

792
01:03:50.543 --> 01:03:52.378
Weights: business owner decides.

793
01:03:52.658 --> 01:03:54.450
Performance or capacity claim:

794
01:03:54.730 --> 01:03:58.741
Model it: latency budget + queueing (L3), then load-test.

795
01:03:59.021 --> 01:04:01.111
Uncertain inputs (demand, price):

796
01:04:01.391 --> 01:04:07.578
Ranges, then Monte Carlo, then expected value, p10 or p90, break-even (L4).

797
01:04:07.858 --> 01:04:10.077
Rule that must hold as code changes:

798
01:04:10.357 --> 01:04:12.447
Fitness function in CI (L2).

799
01:04:13.797 --> 01:04:15.483
Decision's drivers changed:

800
01:04:15.763 --> 01:04:17.875
New ADR superseding the old one.

801
01:04:18.155 --> 01:04:20.352
High uncertainty, similar options:

802
01:04:20.632 --> 01:04:23.448
Prefer the more reversible option; set a trigger.

803
01:04:24.798 --> 01:04:25.929
Exercises.

804
01:04:26.209 --> 01:04:28.897
(B3) Take a real decision from your system.

805
01:04:29.177 --> 01:04:37.369
Write the ADR retroactively: context, three options (incl. status quo), weighted criteria, and a revisit trigger.

806
01:04:37.649 --> 01:04:45.243
(B3) Extend L1 to vary two weights simultaneously (a 2-D grid) and print the region where each option wins.

807
01:04:45.523 --> 01:04:47.273
Is the base point near a boundary?

808
01:04:47.553 --> 01:04:55.297
(B3) Add a fourth fitness function to L2: the api layer may only depend on the application layer (no skipping layers).

809
01:04:55.577 --> 01:04:57.561
Construct a graph that violates it.

810
01:04:58.911 --> 01:05:07.615
(B4) Extend L3 with a third design: parallel fan-out with a 150 ms timeout and a cached fallback for promotions.

811
01:05:07.895 --> 01:05:11.607
How does p99 change, and what does it cost in freshness?

812
01:05:11.887 --> 01:05:20.569
(B4) Change L4's demand distribution to lognormal and add uncertainty to the serverless price (±20%).

813
01:05:20.849 --> 01:05:22.279
Does the decision change?

814
01:05:22.559 --> 01:05:23.988
What's the new break-even?

815
